"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][9]={"university_physics_en:M9-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M9-L1 – Stationary Action and Euler–Lagrange Equations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Analytical Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Derivatives, integration by parts,\nNewtonian mechanics and oscillator energy. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the Euler–Lagrange\nequation from stationary action and apply it to an oscillator.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From Force to Action<\/h2>\n<p>Newtonian dynamics expresses motion through the net force. Analytical\nmechanics starts from a different object: a number assigned to an entire\npath\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbetween two times, called the action. For one degree of freedom,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/msubsup><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S[q]=\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}L(q,\\dot q,t)\\,\\mathrm dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Lagrangian\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas units of energy, while the action\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas units of energy multiplied by time. For many conservative mechanical\nsystems,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=T-V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nkinetic minus potential energy. The physical trajectory is not required\nto be the shortest geometrical path. Instead, small admissible path\nvariations with fixed endpoints leave the action unchanged to first\norder. The construction can be generalized to several coordinates,\nfields and other physical theories.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Variation and Boundary Terms<\/h2>\n<p>Vary the path\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>η<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_\\varepsilon=q+\\varepsilon\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta(t_1)=\\eta(t_2)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe first variation is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>η<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mover><mi>η<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta S=\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}\\left(\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial q}\\eta+\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial\\dot q}\\dot\\eta\\right)\\mathrm dt.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Integration by parts of the second term produces the boundary term\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[(\\partial L/\\partial\\dot q)\\eta]_{t_1}^{t_2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich vanishes for fixed endpoints. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta S=\\int [\\partial L/\\partial q-\\mathrm d(\\partial L/\\partial\\dot q)/\\mathrm dt]\\eta\\,\\mathrm dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince smooth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis arbitrary in the interior,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><menclose notation=\"box\"><mrow><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><\/menclose><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boxed{\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial\\dot q}-\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial q}=0.}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The equation holds for each independent coordinate in a\nmultidimensional system. This derivation assumes sufficient\ndifferentiability and the specified admissible variations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Harmonic Oscillator<\/h2>\n<p>For a mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non an ideal spring of stiffness\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntake\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>k<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=\\frac12m\\dot x^2-\\frac12kx^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial L/\\partial x=-kx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial L/\\partial\\dot x=m\\dot x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEuler–Lagrange gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\ddot x+kx=0,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>the familiar harmonic equation. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=2\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>18<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=18\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=\\sqrt{k/m}=3\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.094<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=2\\pi/3\\,\\mathrm s\\approx2.094\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nInitial data\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(0)=0.1\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x(0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlead to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=0.1\\cos(3t)\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure contrasts a stationary trajectory with nearby trial paths\nallowed in the variation; it is conceptual and does not claim numerical\noptimization of action.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_278_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two possible time paths sharing endpoints with a stationary reference path\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two possible time paths sharing endpoints\nwith a stationary reference path<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The variation keeps both endpoints fixed.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Determine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial L/\\partial\\dot x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=\\frac12m\\dot x^2-V(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Derive the equation of motion for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(x)=mgx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npoints upward.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the angular frequency for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=2\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>18<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=18\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial L/\\partial\\dot x=m\\dot x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe canonical momentum in this example.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial L/\\partial x=-mg<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\ddot x=-mg<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ddot x=-g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>18<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=\\sqrt{18/2}=3\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stationarity Is Not Always a Minimum<\/h2>\n<p>The phrase “least action” is potentially misleading:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta S=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nonly specifies a stationary value. With fixed endpoints, the second\nvariation can be positive, negative or indefinite. The familiar\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=T-V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nform assumes an appropriate conservative model; velocity-dependent\npotentials, time-dependent constraints and dissipation require\nadditional care. Lagrangians are also not unique: adding a total time\nderivative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm dF(q,t)/\\mathrm dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nleaves the equations of motion unchanged with fixed endpoints. The\nobservable content is the trajectory and its dynamics, not the absolute\nnumerical value of an action shifted by a boundary contribution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Varying an entire path yields local dynamics. Constraints will next\nreduce the number of necessary coordinates.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Generalized Coordinates and\nConstraints<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/classical-dynamics/\">David Tong – Classical Dynamics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Individual Chapters, 2 and 4; web line 17; Subject relevance to lesson: M9-L1 – Stationary Action and Euler–Lagrange Equations · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/pages/lecture-notes/\">8.09 Classical Mechanics III – Lecture Notes (2014)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 4; lines 130–135; Subject relevance to lesson: M9-L1 – Stationary Action and Euler–Lagrange Equations · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M9-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M9-L2 – Generalized Coordinates and Constraints<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Analytical Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Euler–Lagrange equations, vector\nkinematics and trigonometry. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Identify holonomic constraints\nand derive pendulum motion using an angle coordinate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Degrees of Freedom Instead of Redundant Coordinates<\/h2>\n<p>An unconstrained point particle moving in a plane requires two\nposition coordinates. Attaching it to an ideal inextensible string of\nlength\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℓ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nimposes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^2+y^2=\\ell^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nleaving one degree of freedom. A holonomic constraint has the form\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(q_1,\\ldots,q_n,t)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand locally permits redundant coordinates to be eliminated. Generalized\ncoordinates need not be distances; angles, relative positions and other\nparameters are allowed. A well-chosen coordinate system is not merely\ncosmetic. For ideal constraints, it can eliminate unknown reaction\nforces from the equations of motion because those reactions do no\nvirtual work along admissible displacements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A Simple Pendulum as a One-Coordinate System<\/h2>\n<p>For a planar pendulum choose angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom the downward vertical. The mass position is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\ell\\sin\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=-\\ell\\cos\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mover><mi>θ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v^2=\\dot x^2+\\dot y^2=\\ell^2\\dot\\theta^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith potential zero at the lowest point,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=mg\\ell(1-\\cos\\theta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mover><mi>θ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=\\tfrac12m\\ell^2\\dot\\theta^2-mg\\ell(1-\\cos\\theta).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Substitution into Euler–Lagrange yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mover><mi>θ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\ell^2\\ddot\\theta+mg\\ell\\sin\\theta=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>θ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mi>g<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ddot\\theta+\\frac g\\ell\\sin\\theta=0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>String tension does not appear even though it enforces circular\nmotion. A Cartesian formulation with a Lagrange multiplier could recover\nthe constraint force.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Period in the Small-Angle Limit<\/h2>\n<p>For small angular deflections measured in radians,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\theta\\approx\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>θ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ddot\\theta+(g/\\ell)\\theta=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msqrt><mfrac><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_0=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac\\ell g}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Taking\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.994<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell=0.994\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=9.81\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_0\\approx2.00\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe ideal bob’s mass cancels. An initial angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.1745<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_0=10^\\circ\\approx0.1745<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrad makes the small-angle approximation reasonably accurate but not\nexact. The figure indicates the circular path, angle coordinate,\ntangential gravity component and radial string tension. Large excursions\nrequire the nonlinear sine equation, whose oscillation period exceeds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_203_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Pendulum on a circular arc showing angle, string tension and gravity\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Pendulum on a circular arc showing angle,\nstring tension and gravity<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Ideal string tension acts along the string.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many degrees of freedom does an ideal planar pendulum have?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell=0.25\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=9.81\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does string tension not enter the angular equation?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">One degree of freedom, such as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>0.25<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9.81<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_0=2\\pi\\sqrt{0.25/9.81}\\approx1.00\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Tension is radial and does no virtual work along the allowed\ntangential displacement.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Real Joints and Nonholonomic Constraints<\/h2>\n<p>Not every constraint can be integrated into an equation involving\npositions alone. Rolling without slipping can introduce\nvelocity-dependent nonholonomic restrictions. When a string breaks, a\ncollision occurs or contact is lost, the set of admissible motions\nchanges, and the original coordinate no longer describes the complete\nmotion. Frictional constraint forces may perform virtual work along\nallowed displacements and require added generalized forces. An angular\ncoordinate is also periodic:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta+2\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresent the same physical orientation despite different coordinate\nvalues. A coordinate choice is useful only within the domain where the\nunderlying mechanical assumptions remain valid.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Generalized coordinates describe admissible motion efficiently.\nSymmetries of that description lead to conserved quantities.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Symmetries, Cyclic Coordinates\nand Noether’s Theorem<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/classical-dynamics/\">David Tong – Classical Dynamics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Individual Chapters, 2 and 4; web line 17; Subject relevance to lesson: M9-L2 – Generalized Coordinates and Constraints · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/pages/lecture-notes/\">8.09 Classical Mechanics III – Lecture Notes (2014)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 4; lines 130–135; Subject relevance to lesson: M9-L2 – Generalized Coordinates and Constraints · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M9-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M9-L3 – Symmetries, Cyclic Coordinates and Noether’s Theorem<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Analytical Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Euler–Lagrange equations, momentum,\nenergy and polar coordinates. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Identify a cyclic coordinate and\nderive the relation between symmetry and conservation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What Remains Unchanged in a System<\/h2>\n<p>Conservation laws are often first learned as separate statements:\nmomentum is constant in the absence of net external force, and angular\nmomentum is constant without torque. Analytical mechanics reveals a\ncommon structure. A continuous transformation that leaves the action\ninvariant under appropriate conditions can possess a conserved quantity.\nAn accessible case occurs when the Lagrangian has no explicit dependence\non one coordinate, called a cyclic coordinate. Noether’s theorem treats\nmore general continuous symmetries, including cases in which the\nLagrangian changes by a total derivative. “Symmetry” means a precisely\ndefined invariance of the mathematical model, not simply a\nregular-looking diagram.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Cyclic Coordinates and Momentum Conservation<\/h2>\n<p>For a generalized coordinate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndefine canonical momentum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_j=\\partial L/\\partial\\dot q_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEuler–Lagrange implies<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot p_j=\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial q_j}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial L/\\partial q_j=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis constant. This is an elementary Noether correspondence. A free\nparticle has Cartesian Lagrangian\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=\\tfrac12m\\dot x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_x=m\\dot x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis conserved. Canonical momentum, however, depends on the chosen\nLagrangian; in electromagnetic potentials it need not coincide with the\nkinetic momentum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\boldsymbol v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSymmetry must therefore be paired with the proper definition of the\ncorresponding conserved quantity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Angular Momentum in a Central Potential<\/h2>\n<p>A mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmoves in a plane under a potential\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthat depends on radius only. In polar coordinates,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mover><mi>r<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=\\frac m2(\\dot r^2+r^2\\dot\\varphi^2)-V(r).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not appear explicitly, hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial L/\\partial\\varphi=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts conjugate momentum is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\varphi=mr^2\\dot\\varphi=\\ell_z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso angular momentum is conserved. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=2\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=3\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot\\varphi=0.5\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell_z=9\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndecreases, angular speed must increase provided no torque acts. The\ndiagram shows the same rotational setting at two angular positions,\nemphasizing invariance about the common center.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_288_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two angular positions of a particle on a circle with one force center\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two angular positions of a particle on a\ncircle with one force center<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Angular momentum is conserved for a central potential.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial L/\\partial q_j=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell_z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=1\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=2\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot\\varphi=3\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Under which condition is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∑<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=\\sum p_j\\dot q_j-L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconserved in an autonomous Lagrangian model?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot p_j=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\ncanonical momentum is constant.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell_z=mr^2\\dot\\varphi=12\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No explicit time dependence of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand validity of the ordinary Euler–Lagrange equations.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Time Translation and Its Assumptions<\/h2>\n<p>For a regular time-independent Lagrangian, the energy function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=\\sum_jp_j\\dot q_j-L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis constant along motion when there is no explicit time dependence:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm dE/\\mathrm dt=-\\partial L/\\partial t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a simple\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=T-V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmodel with quadratic kinetic energy,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T+V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThat identification is not automatic for arbitrary velocity dependence.\nDissipation and external drives can break the assumed symmetries,\ninvalidating an elementary conservation statement. Noether’s conclusion\nalso presumes differentiable continuous transformations and suitable\nboundary and variational conditions. A purely discrete symmetry does not\nby itself generate the same continuous Noether charge.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Rotational invariance provides a conserved quantity. It next\nsimplifies the motion of two gravitating bodies.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Two-Body Problem, Effective\nPotential and Kepler Orbits<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/classical-dynamics/\">David Tong – Classical Dynamics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Individual Chapters, 2 and 4; web line 17; Subject relevance to lesson: M9-L3 – Symmetries, Cyclic Coordinates and Noether’s Theorem · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/pages/lecture-notes/\">8.09 Classical Mechanics III – Lecture Notes (2014)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 4; lines 130–135; Subject relevance to lesson: M9-L3 – Symmetries, Cyclic Coordinates and Noether’s Theorem · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M9-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M9-L4 – Two-Body Problem, Effective Potential and Kepler Orbits<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Analytical Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Newtonian gravity, conservation of\nenergy and angular momentum, polar coordinates. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Reduce the two-body problem to a\nreduced mass and characterize bound central-force orbits.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Relative Motion of Two Masses<\/h2>\n<p>Two freely gravitating bodies mutually influence their common motion.\nInstead of following six Cartesian coordinates directly, separate the\ncenter of mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑹<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol R=(m_1\\boldsymbol r_1+m_2\\boldsymbol r_2)/(m_1+m_2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom relative position\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol r=\\boldsymbol r_1-\\boldsymbol r_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe center of mass of the isolated system moves at constant velocity.\nRelative motion is mathematically equivalent to a single fictitious\nreduced mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=m_1m_2/(m_1+m_2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin potential\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(r)=-Gm_1m_2/r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe reduced mass is not a third physical body but a computational\ndevice. It matters especially when both bodies have comparable masses,\nfor which a fixed-central-body approximation is inadequate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Radial Dynamics and Effective Potential<\/h2>\n<p>Planar relative motion has Lagrangian<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mover><mi>r<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mi>r<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=\\frac\\mu2(\\dot r^2+r^2\\dot\\varphi^2)+\\frac{Gm_1m_2}{r}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The conserved angular momentum magnitude is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell=\\mu r^2\\dot\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSubstituting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot\\varphi=\\ell/(\\mu r^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninto the energy gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><msup><mover><mi>r<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>μ<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mi>r<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=\\frac\\mu2\\dot r^2+V_{\\mathrm{eff}}(r),\\qquad V_{\\mathrm{eff}}(r)=\\frac{\\ell^2}{2\\mu r^2}-\\frac{Gm_1m_2}{r}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The first term is an angular-momentum barrier and the second is\ngravitational attraction. The minimum is at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_0=\\ell^2/(\\mu Gm_1m_2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndefining a stable circular orbit for fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBound negative-energy trajectories in the inverse-square field are\nellipses with the center of force at one focus.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Circular Orbit in Gravity<\/h2>\n<p>When\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≪<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_2\\ll m_1=M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe approximate circular speed at radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=\\sqrt{GM/r}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the orbital period is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msqrt><mfrac><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{r^3}{GM}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.986<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>14<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">GM=3.986\\times10^{14}\\,\\mathrm{m^3/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7.00<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=7.00\\times10^6\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>7.55<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v\\approx7.55\\times10^3\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>5.83<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\approx5.83\\times10^3\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nabout\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>97.1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">97.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nminutes. This is an ideal two-body model with no drag, oblateness or\nperturbing bodies. The figure shows an elliptical orbit with its force\ncenter at a focus and a schematic effective-potential minimum below.\nThese represent different aspects of the same central-force system.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_138_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Elliptic orbit with force center at focus and sketch of effective potential\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Elliptic orbit with force center at focus\nand sketch of effective potential<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Center-of-mass reduction gives a radial equation.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_1=2\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_2=3\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhow does circular orbital period depend on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the areal velocity at fixed angular momentum?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=2\\cdot3/(2+3)=1.2\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\propto r^{3/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\ndoubling radius multiplies period by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.828<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{3/2}\\approx2.828<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>A<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot A=\\ell/(2\\mu)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nindependent of radius.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Orbit Types and Model Limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The Kepler solution presumes Newtonian inverse-square\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∝<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\propto 1/r^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\ngravity between point masses in an isolated two-body system. Finite\nsize, tides, additional gravitating bodies, relativistic corrections and\nnongravitational forces modify the trajectory. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe angular-momentum barrier disappears and radial collisions need\nseparate treatment. Positive-energy trajectories are unbound hyperbolae;\na parabola is the ideal limiting case at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConstant areal velocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>A<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot A=\\ell/(2\\mu)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfollows from any central force, but exact Kepler ellipses specifically\nrequire inverse-square attraction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Center-of-mass reduction and conserved quantities simplify central\nmotion. Linearized motion likewise leads to normal modes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Small Oscillations and Normal\nModes<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/classical-dynamics/\">David Tong – Classical Dynamics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Individual Chapters, 2 and 4; web line 17; Subject relevance to lesson: M9-L4 – Two-Body Problem, Effective Potential and Kepler Orbits · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/pages/lecture-notes/\">8.09 Classical Mechanics III – Lecture Notes (2014)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 4; lines 130–135; Subject relevance to lesson: M9-L4 – Two-Body Problem, Effective Potential and Kepler Orbits · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M9-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M9-L5 – Small Oscillations and Normal Modes<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Analytical Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Taylor expansion, matrix eigenvalues\nand harmonic motion. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Set up linearized motion using\nmass and stiffness matrices and calculate two normal modes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Motion Near an Equilibrium<\/h2>\n<p>A mechanical system with many coordinates generally has coupled\nnonlinear equations of motion. For small displacements\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝜼<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒒<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒒<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol\\eta=\\boldsymbol q-\\boldsymbol q_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom a stable equilibrium\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒒<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol q_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\napproximate the potential by a Taylor expansion. The linear term\nvanishes at equilibrium, while the Hessian of the potential determines\nlocal stiffness. Inertia is represented by a mass matrix. Normal-mode\nanalysis resolves the coupled small motions into characteristic\nindependent patterns. A normal mode is a fixed spatial pattern whose\namplitude oscillates harmonically in time. The method explains molecular\nvibrations, mechanical resonators and elastic structures within the\nlinear regime.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mass and Stiffness Matrices<\/h2>\n<p>For small displacements the Lagrangian is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><msup><mover><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝜼<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><mi>M<\/mi><mover><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝜼<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝜼<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><mi>K<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝜼<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=\\tfrac12\\dot{\\boldsymbol\\eta}^{\\mathsf T}M\\dot{\\boldsymbol\\eta}-\\tfrac12\\boldsymbol\\eta^{\\mathsf T}K\\boldsymbol\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith symmetric\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEuler–Lagrange yields<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mover><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝜼<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝜼<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\ddot{\\boldsymbol\\eta}+K\\boldsymbol\\eta=0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A harmonic trial solution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝜼<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol\\eta=\\boldsymbol a\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(K-\\omega^2M)\\boldsymbol a=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNonzero amplitudes exist when<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det(K-\\omega^2M)=0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For positive-definite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand positive-semidefinite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsquared eigenfrequencies are nonnegative. Under appropriate conditions\nthe mode vectors can be chosen orthogonal in the mass inner product. A\nzero mode corresponds to marginal or free motion, not a positive\noscillation frequency.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Two Coupled Masses<\/h2>\n<p>Two equal masses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare attached to outer springs of stiffness\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand connected to each other by a spring of stiffness\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>κ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor displacements\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1,x_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom equilibrium,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>κ<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mi>κ<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mi>κ<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>κ<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=m\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad K=\\begin{pmatrix}k+\\kappa&amp;-\\kappa\\\\-\\kappa&amp;k+\\kappa\\end{pmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The in-phase mode\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol a_+=(1,1)^\\mathsf T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not stretch the coupling spring, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_+^2=k/m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe out-of-phase mode\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol a_-=(1,-1)^\\mathsf T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstretches it, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>κ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_-^2=(k+2\\kappa)/m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=1\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=4\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa=3\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe angular frequencies are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_+=2\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>10<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_-=\\sqrt{10}\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure compares the two displacement patterns rather than two\ndifferent apparatuses.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_057_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two coupled spring masses moving in-phase and out-of-phase\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two coupled spring masses moving in-phase\nand out-of-phase<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The coupling spring responds only to relative\ndisplacement.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What equation determines the eigenfrequencies?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_+<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=2\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=8\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does positive coupling stiffness\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>κ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naffect the out-of-phase mode?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det(K-\\omega^2M)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_+=\\sqrt{8/2}=2\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It increases\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_-^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>κ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\kappa/m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative to the in-phase mode.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stability and Limits of Linearization<\/h2>\n<p>Linearization assumes sufficiently small excursions around the chosen\nequilibrium and, in this example, ideal massless linear springs. At\nlarge displacements, nonlinear coupling may exchange energy between\nmodes, invalidating a fixed normal-mode solution. Negative eigenvalues\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M^{-1}K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nproduce exponential growth, indicating linear instability rather than a\nreal oscillation frequency. Damping modifies the modes, and\nnonproportional damping can require complex eigenvalues. Zero modes must\nbe interpreted carefully: they may arise from symmetry, missing supports\nor a marginally stable configuration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Normal modes emerge from a linear eigenvalue problem. The same\ndynamics can instead be expressed using position and momentum.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Hamiltonian Mechanics and Phase\nSpace<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/classical-dynamics/\">David Tong – Classical Dynamics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Individual Chapters, 2 and 4; web line 17; Subject relevance to lesson: M9-L5 – Small Oscillations and Normal Modes · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/pages/lecture-notes/\">8.09 Classical Mechanics III – Lecture Notes (2014)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 4; lines 130–135; Subject relevance to lesson: M9-L5 – Small Oscillations and Normal Modes · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M9-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M9-L6 – Hamiltonian Mechanics and Phase Space<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Analytical Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Lagrangian mechanics, partial\nderivatives, energy and differential equations. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Perform the Legendre transform\nand obtain Hamilton’s equations and oscillator phase space.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>States from Coordinates and Momenta<\/h2>\n<p>A second-order equation for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be replaced by two coupled first-order equations. Hamiltonian\nmechanics represents the state as a point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(q,p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin phase space. Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the canonical momentum conjugate to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot necessarily mass times velocity in every model. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent degrees of freedom, phase space has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndimensions. Time evolution traces a trajectory in this space, making\ninitial values and conserved quantities easier to visualize. The\nformulation is especially useful in statistical mechanics, celestial\ndynamics and as a mathematical bridge to quantum theory.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Legendre Transform and Canonical Equations<\/h2>\n<p>Set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_i=\\partial L/\\partial\\dot q_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand assume that these relations can be solved locally for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot q_i(q,p,t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Legendre transform is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(q,p,t)=\\sum_i p_i\\dot q_i-L(q,\\dot q,t).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Upon differentiation, terms containing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm d\\dot q_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncancel by the momentum definition. Together with Euler–Lagrange\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot p_i=\\partial L/\\partial q_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none obtains<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot q_i=\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial p_i},\\qquad\\dot p_i=-\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial q_i}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Along a canonical trajectory,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm dH/\\mathrm dt=\\partial H/\\partial t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus a time-independent regular Hamiltonian\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis conserved. Whether its numerical value equals the physical total\nenergy requires the standard conservative assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Elliptical Phase-Space Trajectory<\/h2>\n<p>For the harmonic oscillator,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>k<\/mi><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=\\tfrac12m\\dot q^2-\\tfrac12kq^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=m\\dot q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Hamiltonian is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=\\frac{p^2}{2m}+\\frac{kq^2}{2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Hamilton’s equations yield\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot q=p/m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot p=-kq<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\ddot q+kq=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt positive energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe orbit satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p^2/(2mE)+kq^2/(2E)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nan ellipse in the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nplane. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=1\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=4\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand amplitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=0.5\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe energy is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>k<\/mi><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=\\tfrac12kA^2=0.5\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{\\max}=m\\omega A=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe picture depicts a closed phase-space ellipse with labeled\nintercepts, not the oscillator mass’s spatial trajectory.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_123_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Elliptic phase-space trajectory with position and momentum axes\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Elliptic phase-space trajectory with\nposition and momentum axes<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Position and momentum amplitudes are the semiaxes.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=\\tfrac12m\\dot q^2-V(q)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{\\max}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin SI units and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=0.5\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the phase-space dimension of a system with three degrees of\nfreedom?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=m\\dot q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=p^2/(2m)+V(q)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=2\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{\\max}=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Six: three position variables and three canonical momenta.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Regularity and Time Dependence<\/h2>\n<p>The Legendre transform requires a sufficiently regular velocity\nHessian\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>∂<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial^2L/\\partial\\dot q_i\\partial\\dot q_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSingular cases, including some gauge systems, impose constraints and\nneed an extended treatment. Explicitly time-dependent Hamiltonians may\nexchange energy with external driving. A damped nonconservative\noscillator shows a shrinking phase portrait, whereas the canonical\ndynamics considered here preserves phase-space volume. The orientation\nand shape of the diagram also depend on the chosen canonical\ncoordinates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Hamilton’s equations organize trajectories in phase space. Canonical\ntransformations preserve the structure of those equations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Poisson Brackets and Canonical\nTransformations<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/classical-dynamics/\">David Tong – Classical Dynamics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Individual Chapters, 2 and 4; web line 17; Subject relevance to lesson: M9-L6 – Hamiltonian Mechanics and Phase Space · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/pages/lecture-notes/\">8.09 Classical Mechanics III – Lecture Notes (2014)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 4; lines 130–135; Subject relevance to lesson: M9-L6 – Hamiltonian Mechanics and Phase Space · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M9-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M9-L7 – Poisson Brackets and Canonical Transformations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Analytical Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Hamilton’s equations, partial\nderivatives and Jacobian matrices. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Evaluate the Poisson bracket and\ntest canonical structure for a momentum shift.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Changing Coordinates Without Losing Dynamics<\/h2>\n<p>Not every change of variables in phase space is equivalent. We seek\ntransformations\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>↦<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(q,p)\\mapsto(Q,P)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthat preserve the canonical form of Hamilton’s equations. These are\ncalled canonical or symplectic transformations. They can simplify the\ndynamical description without changing its physical content. The Poisson\nbracket is a basic tool for analyzing such transformations. It combines\nfunctions on phase space and also describes their evolution along\nHamiltonian trajectories. The symmetric appearance of position and\nmomentum equations reflects a specific geometric structure that an\narbitrary nonlinear substitution can destroy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Definition and Basic Identities<\/h2>\n<p>For one canonical pair the Poisson bracket is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{f,g\\}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial q}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial p}-\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial p}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial q}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For multiple degrees of freedom sum over\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDirect calculation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{q,p\\}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{q,q\\}=\\{p,p\\}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{f,g\\}=-\\{g,f\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAlong a Hamiltonian trajectory, any quantity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(q,p,t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsatisfies<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm df}{\\mathrm dt}=\\{f,H\\}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A time-independent\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis conserved when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{f,H\\}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA regular local transformation is canonical when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>δ<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{Q_i,P_j\\}=\\delta_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{Q_i,Q_j\\}=\\{P_i,P_j\\}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Canonical Shear<\/h2>\n<p>Consider the transformation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=p+\\alpha q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts derivatives are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial Q/\\partial q=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial Q/\\partial p=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial P/\\partial q=\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial P/\\partial p=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTherefore<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{Q,P\\}=1\\cdot1-0\\cdot\\alpha=1.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The figure shows how a small rectangle in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(q,p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncoordinates becomes a parallelogram under this shear. Its area is\nunchanged because the Jacobian determinant is one. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin compatible units, point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(q,p)=(1,3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmaps to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(Q,P)=(1,5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe inverse is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=P-\\alpha Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nallowing the Hamiltonian to be re-expressed in the new canonical\ncoordinates.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_036_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Rectangle in original phase space and equal-area parallelogram in transformed phase space\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Rectangle in original phase space and\nequal-area parallelogram in transformed phase space<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>A rectangle becomes an equal-area parallelogram.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{q^2,p\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Verify\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{Q,P\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=p+3q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Where does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(q,p)=(2,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmap for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=p+3q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{q^2,p\\}=2q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{Q,P\\}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso the transformation is canonical.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(Q,P)=(2,7)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Structural Preservation and Caveats<\/h2>\n<p>Area preservation alone does not characterize a general\ntransformation as symplectic when there is more than one degree of\nfreedom: the full symplectic structure must be preserved. In a\ntwo-dimensional phase space, orientation-preserving area preservation\nhas a particularly close relation to canonicity. Time-dependent\ncanonical transformations alter the new Hamiltonian by additional terms.\nA Poisson bracket is not an ordinary vector cross product; it has\nbilinearity, a product rule and the Jacobi identity. Liouville’s\nphase-volume conservation applies to sufficiently regular Hamiltonian\nflows, not to generic frictional dissipative dynamics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Poisson brackets describe symplectic evolution. Hamilton–Jacobi\ntheory seeks an especially useful canonical description.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Hamilton–Jacobi Equation and\nAction Variables<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/classical-dynamics/\">David Tong – Classical Dynamics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Individual Chapters, 2 and 4; web line 17; Subject relevance to lesson: M9-L7 – Poisson Brackets and Canonical Transformations · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/pages/lecture-notes/\">8.09 Classical Mechanics III – Lecture Notes (2014)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 4; lines 130–135; Subject relevance to lesson: M9-L7 – Poisson Brackets and Canonical Transformations · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M9-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M9-L8 – Hamilton–Jacobi Equation and Action Variables<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Analytical Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Hamiltonian, Poisson brackets,\nintegration and harmonic oscillator. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Formulate the Hamilton–Jacobi\nequation and compute the harmonic oscillator action variable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dynamics in a Characteristic Function<\/h2>\n<p>Hamilton’s equations are ordinary differential equations for\ncoordinates and momenta. An alternative approach is to find a function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(q,t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhose coordinate derivative gives momentum. This principal or\nHamilton–Jacobi action function can serve as a generating function for a\ncanonical transformation to simpler variables. The method is more than a\nchange in notation: in integrable systems it provides a route to\nperiodic motion, action-angle coordinates and later semiclassical\napproximations. Conceptually, the function of endpoint and time used\nhere must be distinguished from the path functional\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S[q]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nintroduced in the first lesson.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hamilton–Jacobi and Separation<\/h2>\n<p>Using the convention\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=\\partial S/\\partial q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Hamilton–Jacobi equation is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial S}{\\partial t}+H\\left(q,\\frac{\\partial S}{\\partial q},t\\right)=0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For an autonomous one-dimensional system try\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>;<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(q,t)=W(q;E)-Et<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe characteristic function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>W<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthen satisfies<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H\\left(q,\\frac{\\mathrm dW}{\\mathrm dq}\\right)=E.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=p^2/(2m)+V(q)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm dW/\\mathrm dq=\\pm\\sqrt{2m[E-V(q)]}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin classically allowed regions. Integration over\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>W<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nup to a constant. A complete solution with appropriate integration\nconstants can reconstruct the trajectories. Turning points require\nchanges of square-root branch; one branch alone does not describe a full\nperiodic orbit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Harmonic Oscillator Action Variable<\/h2>\n<p>For a closed phase-space orbit define the action variable<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>∮<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\oint p\\,\\mathrm dq.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For the oscillator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=p^2/(2m)+m\\omega^2q^2/2=E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe orbit is an ellipse with semiaxes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>E<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_{\\max}=\\sqrt{2E/(m\\omega^2)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{\\max}=\\sqrt{2mE}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts enclosed area is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi q_{\\max}p_{\\max}=2\\pi E/\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>E<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=\\frac{E}{\\omega}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=1\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=2\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=0.5\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=0.25\\,\\mathrm{J\\,s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure displays the ellipse and its area\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt visualizes an action variable rather than asserting a new energy\nlaw.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_002_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Ellipse of the oscillator showing the enclosed action area\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Ellipse of the oscillator showing the\nenclosed action area<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The enclosed area equals 2πJ.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">State the Hamilton–Jacobi equation for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=p^2/(2m)+V(q)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=1.2\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=3\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the enclosed area of the corresponding\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nphase-space ellipse?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>S<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/msub><mi>S<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_tS+(\\partial_qS)^2/(2m)+V(q)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=E/\\omega=0.4\\,\\mathrm{J\\,s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.513<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=2\\pi J=0.8\\pi\\,\\mathrm{J\\,s}\\approx2.513\\,\\mathrm{J\\,s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scope and Open Systems<\/h2>\n<p>Action-angle variables are especially effective for sufficiently\nintegrable bound motions that can be decomposed into appropriate\nperiodic cycles. Chaotic, open and strongly nonintegrable systems\ngenerally lack global action-angle coordinates. Even integrable models\nmay require local treatment near turning points, singularities and\nmultivalued functions. Under slowly varying parameters,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be approximately adiabatically invariant when additional conditions\nhold, but resonance and separatrix crossings can invalidate the\napproximation. The later appearance of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℏ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hbar<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin quantum mechanics does not mean that the present classical integral\nis quantized without further assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Hamilton–Jacobi theory completes the transition from Newton’s force\nlaw to a geometric phase-space picture and prepares subsequent dynamical\nsystems and quantum theory.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/classical-dynamics/\">David Tong – Classical Dynamics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Individual Chapters, 2 and 4; web line 17; Subject relevance to lesson: M9-L8 – Hamilton–Jacobi Equation and Action Variables · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-09-classical-mechanics-iii-fall-2014/pages/lecture-notes/\">8.09 Classical Mechanics III – Lecture Notes (2014)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 4; lines 130–135; Subject relevance to lesson: M9-L8 – Hamilton–Jacobi Equation and Action Variables · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
